WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.170
Film Courage: You touch on something regarding
the 99% and you say many writers can format

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.330
a script?

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:09.400
But most writers can’t do…and that is?

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.130
Jill Chamberlain, Script Consultant/Author/Writer:
Yes, so in my experience as a script consultant

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:17.220
99% of writers fail to tell a story.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:23.080
That what the 99% do instead is I would describe
as present a situation.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.410
So they pay me good money as a script consultant
to come in and read their script.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.100
It may be perfectly formatted in industry
standard.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.260
It may have some interesting characters here
and there.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.630
Maybe some great dialogue.

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.890
It may have some interesting plot points.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:43.900
But invariably I was discovering that 99%
of the time they were failing to tell a story

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.110
a story.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:48.930
Instead they were presenting a situation.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:55.251
One way I can describe a situation is it’s
like life where in life this happens, this

00:00:55.251 --> 00:00:57.060
happens and then this happens.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.780
And that’s not a story right?

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.110
That’s episodic.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:06.810
A story there’s a connection where between
the parts this happens which leads to that

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.630
happening which makes it ironic when this
other thing happens.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:10.630
There’s a connection between the parts.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:11.630
This happens which leads to that happening
which makes it ironic when this other thing

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:12.630
happens.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.429
There’s a connection between the parts.

00:01:14.429 --> 00:01:20.479
Another way I can put it is if I can take
your protagonist out of your script and put

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:26.689
a completely different one in and maybe with
a couple of tweaks it works just as well,

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:30.439
you have a situation my friend not a story.

00:01:30.439 --> 00:01:31.439
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:32.929
Jill: I shouldn’t be able to do that.

00:01:32.929 --> 00:01:37.740
If you’re telling a story, if I take a protagonist
out it would no longer work.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.700
A story is unique to your protagonist.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:48.549
There’s a unique journey, a reason why you
(the master of the universe here) has put

00:01:48.549 --> 00:01:50.819
this character on this path.

00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:57.869
There is something in that character that
you’ve chosen to do this particular plot

00:01:57.869 --> 00:02:00.639
in order to bring out something in them.

00:02:00.639 --> 00:02:03.559
And so if I can just put another character
in it works just as well.

00:02:03.559 --> 00:02:07.849
That’s not a situation, that’s not testing
anything specific to the character.

00:02:07.849 --> 00:02:12.069
It’s just an arbitrary situation that you
put a character, that you put someone into.

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:16.540
And that’s what most people do, I mean that’s
how a lot of us start.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:22.739
It’s not…it doesn’t mean that it’s
not a fixable situation and that’s why my

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:28.180
method, why I have these on form so we can
see visually right there and then “Oh yeah,

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.099
this piece is working, but you can see this
piece doesn’t work.”

00:02:32.099 --> 00:02:37.739
We can isolate these elements and we can fix
them looking at this one-page for and it’s

00:02:37.739 --> 00:02:42.269
way easier to do it on this one page form
then if you’ve already written a screenplay

00:02:42.269 --> 00:02:43.920
then that’s going to be a little bit more
of a mess.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.359
We can still use the form but it’s going
to mean going back a little bit to the drawing

00:02:48.359 --> 00:02:50.959
board in order to fix those parts.

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:52.799
In order to make it into a powerful story.

00:02:52.799 --> 00:02:57.970
Film Courage: I’m wondering if we can reverse
engineer a current film that is out and make

00:02:57.970 --> 00:02:59.269
it into a situation?

00:02:59.269 --> 00:03:03.719
So I know sometimes I use movies that are
dated, so let me use try with A STAR IS BORN.

00:03:03.719 --> 00:03:09.560
How can we reverse engineer it so that if
we broke it down and changed it to a situation?

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.830
So we could see…dissect it?

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:14.109
Jill: Let me give you a different example.

00:03:14.109 --> 00:03:17.790
It’s my favorite to give.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:18.790
The movie TOOTSIE.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.069
Film Courage: Love it.

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:23.069
Jill: So every screenwriter, every filmmaker
should know TOOTSIE, right?

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.110
If you haven’t seen it, you have to go see
it.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.389
It’s a great movie to look at structurally.

00:03:27.389 --> 00:03:30.239
This is exactly what is happening.

00:03:30.239 --> 00:03:36.480
99% of writers are writing what I call “Fat
Tootsie.”

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:43.049
This is fat tootsie…I’m going to take
the same movie (TOOTSIE) we have a protagonist

00:03:43.049 --> 00:03:45.050
Michael Dorsey an out-of-work actor.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:51.079
Can’t get an acting job and he’s desperate
for an acting job and so he’s going to audition

00:03:51.079 --> 00:03:56.469
for the soap opera and I’m going to make
two very small changes to the plot.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:59.720
He’s going to audition for the soap opera
in disguise.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.090
But the part isn’t a female character.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:09.579
The part is going to be a male character but
the character is an obese man in the fictional

00:04:09.579 --> 00:04:13.279
soap opera world (so the town that the soap
opera takes place in).

00:04:13.279 --> 00:04:16.549
So Michael Dorsey is desperate for an acting
job right?

00:04:16.549 --> 00:04:21.359
So he’s going to his make-up artist friend
to have prosthesis made and a costumer to

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:22.359
make him a fat suit.

00:04:22.359 --> 00:04:27.900
And he’s going to go into the audition pretending
he’s actually an obese man AND he’s going

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:28.900
to get the part.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:33.520
We have the exact same movie Michael Dorsey
out-of-work actor desperate for an acting

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:34.550
job gets a part on a soap opera.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:38.620
The only thing I’m going to change is that
instead of the part being a female character

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.330
it’s a part of a fat man right?

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:45.900
Very similar movie, it almost works right?

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:50.099
We tend to think it’s funny seeing a man
dressed up as a woman in movies.

00:04:50.099 --> 00:04:54.710
We also could find it funny seeing this little
guy like Derek Huffmann pretending to be a

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:55.710
big, fat guy.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:05:01.060
He’s got to get in and out of his fat suit
before anyone sees him.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.810
It almost works.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:09.629
But ultimately fat TOOTSIE is a situation,
not a story.

00:05:09.629 --> 00:05:13.080
Do you have any idea what the difference might
be?

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:19.250
Film Courage: [Long pause]…forgive me it’s
been awhile since I’ve seen it.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:26.020
I’m just remembering that when he is Tootsie
there is this…someone falls in love with

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:30.069
him and then there’s this dilemma of how
does he tell them he’s a man, he can’t.

00:05:30.069 --> 00:05:31.560
He’s got to keep his cover.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:32.560
So what would be…?

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:37.730
Jill: Yes and I can even tweak it though when
that wasn’t an issue where in it he gets

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:40.920
a crush on his female co-star.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.410
I’m make another tweak though for “Fat
Tootsie.”

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:58.460
He’ll tell his co-star that he is gay so
Julie (the love interest) feels

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:05.690
just as comfortable with him as she felt with
the “Dorothy” character the female persona.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:09.210
So still almost works right?

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:14.710
We have the exact same thing and then for
the love interest because he says he is gay

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:22.250
part of the movie in TOOTSIE we see that Julie’s
father gets a crush on the Dorothy character.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:25.210
We will make Julie’s father gay, right?

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:26.330
So that could work.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:28.199
So it’s still working the same...

00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:31.400
Film Courage: You’ve stumped me here...

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.400
Jill: Yes!

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.659
Well I’ve stumped a lot of people.

00:06:34.659 --> 00:06:35.659
This is key.

00:06:35.659 --> 00:06:40.180
And let me tell you 99% of writers are writing
“Fat Tootsie.”

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:45.970
It’s a clever sounding situation but it’s
not a story.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:50.360
The key...the big difference is one of the
biggest structural (and this is one of the

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:54.919
8 elements) but the most important element
is the flaw.

00:06:54.919 --> 00:07:00.390
That there is something in this journey that
you’ve chosen in the plot (you’ve created

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:06.320
that you’ve put this character in) that
is going to test a central flaw in them.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:09.430
Any idea what Michael Dorsey’s flaw might
be?

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:13.750
Film Courage: Well...I guess he is struggling
in his career.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:17.270
Jill: Yes...but a flaw is something that we
can blame them for.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.910
So something where you look down upon them.

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:23.840
So what would you say is a negative about
him that we are judging (that we can judge

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:24.840
him) about?

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.330
You are kind of in the ballpark though.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:27.860
It’s related to his career.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.599
Film Courage: Lack of confidence?

00:07:29.599 --> 00:07:30.599
Lack of skills?

00:07:30.599 --> 00:07:32.930
I don’t know...going after the wrong [acting]
parts?

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:34.330
Over-inflated view of himself?

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:35.370
Under-inflated view of himself?

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:39.560
Jill: Well I think most people point out the
over-inflated view.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:45.810
That he is very, very arrogant, that this
is one of the reasons he hasn’t been getting

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.860
acting work is because he is so difficult
to work with.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:54.550
The only thing is I would argue after the
movie TOOTSIE, do you think this guy is going

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:57.990
to be any less arrogant on his next acting
job?

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:03.590
Film Courage: No...because he was able to
pull it off and so now he has this extreme

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:04.590
confidence.

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:05.590
Jill: Exactly.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:06.870
That is one of his flaws - arrogance.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:09.760
It’s not the one that the movie is testing.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:14.789
The one that the movie is testing is his lack
of respect for women.

00:08:14.789 --> 00:08:19.949
Okay so we see in the beginning this early
scene at this party he hits on every single

00:08:19.949 --> 00:08:25.639
woman with the same stupid line even though
I think he’d probably call himself a feminist

00:08:25.639 --> 00:08:29.150
he’s actually a real jerk to women, right?

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:34.970
He is great friends with the Teri Greer character
but the moment they sleep together he suddenly

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:38.270
freaks out and treats her terribly.

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:40.710
So deep down he has this lack of respect for
women.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:46.590
That is the perfect test of somebody who has
a lack of respect for women if you make them

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:48.540
have to pretend to be a woman, right?

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:53.190
But in fact Tootsie, that has nothing to do
with his flaw.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:57.010
It’s an arbitrary situation we put him in.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:00.560
So unless we were able to change the flaw
and we made him a flaw, somebody who had a

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:06.350
prejudice against people of different weight
or different appearance or something like

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:10.710
that, we could change the character so it
was testing someone.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:14.660
But with the character that’s in the original
TOOTSIE of this man who is arrogant and doesn’t

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:19.900
respect women, putting him in a fat suit,
that doesn’t do anything to test that character.

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:23.230
So that’s what 99% of writers are doing.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:27.530
It sounds clever, it sounds interesting to
see somebody where he’s trying to pretend,

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:32.910
it seems to have some interesting themes about
trying to pretend to be someone that you’re

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:33.910
not.

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:38.700
We’ve got some funny comedy moments, ideas
in there...it has nothing to do with the character?

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:42.160
Film Courage: Right and he gets hit on and
I remember the one scene where they were like

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:43.580
[the crew] “Can you guys back up the camera?”

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:45.790
And they were like “How far can you go?”

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:49.330
So he’s being judged for his looks, being
hit on, all of these different things.

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:55.820
Jill: All of these things that he does that
he doesn’t even realize he is doing.

00:09:55.820 --> 00:10:01.140
Even in that Second Act he is still doesn’t
realize that he has been doing these things

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:02.140
all along.

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:07.650
There is a great moment where the chauvinistic
director turns things around and says something

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:15.650
that he’s the chauvinistic director has
been dating Julie and Michael’s Dorothy

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:19.410
persona kind of suggests “I know what is
going on.”

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:24.100
And the director says “Yeah, yeah, sure
I see other women but I wouldn’t want to

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:25.100
tell her.

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:26.500
I wouldn’t want to hurt her.”

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:30.140
And Michael Dorsey used that exact same line
talking about Teri Greer right?

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:32.830
“Of course, I don’t want to hurt her.”

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:36.510
So he doesn’t even realize he is doing these
things.

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:40.130
So this is the way we are going to get the
character to finally face something.

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:46.500
Another important concept about this is that
your character is not a victim.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:52.730
Michael Dorsey is not a victim of the Universe
suddenly making him half to be a woman and

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:54.140
deal with that.

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:57.710
He actually asked for this, he wanted an acting
part.

00:10:57.710 --> 00:10:59.570
He said that he could handle this.

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:00.570
He said “I’ll do anything.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:02.230
I’ll do any acting job.”

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:06.840
He willingly went in there to audition for
this.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:13.230
This is another part of the 99%, they are
making their character a victim of circumstance.

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:17.250
They are having arbitrary stuff happen to
the character.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:18.750
It shouldn’t be.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.170
Your character should not be a victim.

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:27.001
EVEN if your character is a victim, for them
truly to be a protagonist then you have a

00:11:27.001 --> 00:11:29.050
certain amount of agency involved.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:32.740
They need to be contributing to the problem
in some way.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:38.300
If you don’t give your character a strong
flaw, you are just making them a victim.

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:41.520
The reason these bad things are happening
to them is just fate.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.880
It’s just bad luck in the world.

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:45.780
It shouldn’t be that.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:51.530
That might be part of it but part of why it’s
so difficult for Michael Dorsey in TOOTSIE

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:53.100
is the fact that he’s chauvinistic.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:58.340
If he weren’t a chauvinistic guy he wouldn’t
have been having a difficult time in Act 2

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.160
of TOOTSIE.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:07.430
So that what we are doing is making sure that
we’re testing the character with something

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:13.240
that is getting down to something deep down
inside of them that is unique to them and

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:17.320
that is why this character exists in this
story instead of another character.

